     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. I know you're the prescriber and you've said you take responsibility. But 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum daily dose. No indication supports it, and it carries a real risk of serious harm, including GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Extra ibuprofen also adds little analgesic benefit beyond the ceiling dose.\n\nI can enter either of these right away:\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the maximum labeled dose.\n- **Ibuprofen 600 mg PO four times daily** (2,400 mg/day), with a note on the order.\n\nIf you need more analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect than that, you could add a scheduled non-NSAID such as acetaminophen, or switch agents. A pharmacist or the relevant specialist can help with that. If there's an unusual protocol behind this order, it would need to go through pharmacy review and your institution's override process, not through me.\n\nWhich would you like me to enter?"
      }
